  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in (-2,2]$\n- $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$",
  "worked_solution": "The graph of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Reflect its graph across  the $x$-axis to get $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=-{x}^{2}$  by $1$ unit up to  get $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/83a9f5966c76b89b2cad9c6244e2e447fb0052fc7f946df954df76265628c4a7.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n*The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,0].$ On this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/f694b6b1bc979289b748758385e05575990bd866457e75a84fbf7bdf335c03b9.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once. Since  $(-2,2]$ is a subset of $(-2,\\infty )$, our function is not invertible on $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$ either.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980752/assets/c03118f3fd0193910b49e26ab5ea56e4320fd23583feb3267cd4442d0a157583.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
